WEBVTT

00:00:00.621 --> 00:00:05.072
Run the monthly order project and verify the row meaning.

00:00:05.072 --> 00:00:08.602
Each row now represents one month, not one order.

00:00:08.602 --> 00:00:12.927
The grouped measures summarize all order events in that month.

00:00:12.927 --> 00:00:18.132
This is a good example of using temporal preparation to create an analysis-ready table at a new grain.

00:00:18.132 --> 00:00:23.472
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:23.472 --> 00:00:29.962
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:29.962 --> 00:00:33.487
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:33.487 --> 00:00:39.432
In a real setting such as a CRM standardizing names, emails, and phone numbers,

00:00:39.432 --> 00:00:46.127
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:46.127 --> 00:00:48.197
before discussing syntax.

00:00:48.197 --> 00:00:53.840
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
